A||||11||Songman||Hey Aus! Widebody?||||5||
Z||000000||Songman||05-23-2001||01:26 AM||||Hey Aus...<P>I love all the stuff you are doing so far and can't wait for it to be all available...<P>I have wondered this before but since someone brought up the Pyrahna.... what about some sort of widebody for the Fiero? Everyone seems to like the IMSA kit, but why not something more like on a 911 or something... the Pyrahna has something along that line on the back.... something that looks like part of the car but without being tacky... <P>May be too much to ask. That is a tall order..<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.laura.ru/photo/laura3_1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||AusFiero||05-23-2001||02:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B><BR>May be too much to ask. That is a tall order..<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe, maybe not Songman.<BR>You have me thinking now. If I am going to release mix and match body kits maybe I can do that with my moulds too. You know, bolt section A to section B for wide body effect or bolt section A to section C for just scoops. I know the skirts would be easy to do. The widebody from the back is the hard section, without just making a new bumper that is. <P>Has anyone found a decent adhesive in America to bond Fiero bodies and fibreglass together? We have one here that will stick fibreglass to a wet fish if you wanted to and it would not come off.  Was wondering what you use there?<P>Will rack my brain on it as I always thought my fastback would look good with subtle bulges in the right spots.<P>God I need another Fiero body to just play around on. I am not looking forward to 3 to 6 months now off the road as I have started to make the plugs for the body kit. Well make is a little innacurate, I do have at this stage some lovely drawings and measurements on the garage floor and measurments marked on my car though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Basically Songman, if all my other products sell well and their is a demand at that stage, ask and ye shall probably receive.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero.gif"> <BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||05-23-2001||03:00 AM||||Very cool! I figured that was about what your answer would be....<P>Wish I knew a little something about fiberglass and body work now so I could help you.. but I don't... I'll watch with interest.<P>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||05-23-2001||08:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B> The widebody from the back is the hard section, without just making a new bumper that is. <P>Has anyone found a decent adhesive in America to bond Fiero bodies and fiberglass together? We have one here that will stick fiberglass to a wet fish if you wanted to and it would not come off.  Was wondering what you use there?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Aus,<P>Regarding the bumper. Since it's fairly flexible, I believe you could use the factory bumper and just move the mounting location of the lower quarters (the portion of the bumper under the side marker light) outward. Probably as much as two inches without effecting anything.<P>As far as the "fish" glue. I dunno. But, as a spin off of your first drawings showing a smooth sided Fiero. If you combined the wide body kit with new door skins, I believe you could effectively remove the molding everywhere but the front and rear bumpers. <P>Just a couple of thoughts,<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||05-23-2001||08:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B> <BR>God I need another Fiero body to just play around on. I am not looking forward to 3 to 6 months now off the road as I have started to make the plugs for the body kit. Well make is a little inaccurate, I do have at this stage some lovely drawings and measurements on the garage floor and measurements marked on my car though  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I guess this is a crazy idea but I have read how expensive it is to get a Fiero over there with fees and taxes.  What if we all send you a Fiero piece by piece so you would have a prototype car to work with. If I send a few parts that I have and everyone else sends a part before long you would have a complete car. I know the chassis would be the hardest to do. It would be a cool project.  With what you would be offering the Fiero community I would contribute.  <P><P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL><BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pictures-1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||AusFiero||05-23-2001||10:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by skitime:<BR><B> I guess this is a crazy idea but I have read how expensive it is to get a Fiero over there with fees and taxes.  What if we all send you a Fiero piece by piece so you would have a prototype car to work with. If I send a few parts that I have and everyone else sends a part before long you would have a complete car. I know the chassis would be the hardest to do. It would be a cool project.  With what you would be offering the Fiero community I would contribute.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmm I have a friend on the USS Chosin that bought some parts over for me recently. Saved me like $800.  He has a fair bit of room on the ship available too. And all I really need to get hold of is a notchback rear end as I don't mind my car being off the road to badly...look what it will be when finished. I could then develop the notchback pieces easily by mocking up a fake rear body section on a jig and working with it instead of my car. The other bits I dont mind developing on my car because pictures dont show it due to the shine but my paint isnt work spitting on in my books, so damaging it doesn't matter.<P>Yeah expensive is an understatement Skitime. I paid $14000 for mine in pitiful condition. Only because it was a t-topped car and a GT.<BR>Can buy a cheap left hand drive average condition 4cyl coupe for about $5000 if you are lucky.<P>Although I do know where there is an nice fixer up project which I hope stays there a while...has been now. A guy I know has an 88GT Fastback, factory t-topped car needing restoration totally but already RHD for only $6000 Aus or about $3000 US.<P>Will consider that possibility Skitime after the side scoops are on the market...that way I may be able to offer people a discount to people in appreciation for sending some body bits over parts.<P>Will definately look into shipping it via US Gov shipping lines if I can contact Ben on the Chosin again. I think he comes here too...Are you out there Ben?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||AusFiero||05-23-2001||10:46 AM||||Good idea too MrFixit...Of course the bumper will bend more than enough needed for a wide body to taper back in.||||reg||14
